[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is TeslaMate, a free, open-source, self-hosted data logger for your Tesla. Your car generates a gold mine of data every single day. Drives, charges, efficiency, battery health. Tesla\'s app shows you a sliver of it and forgets the rest within days. TeslaMate remembers'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all of it, every mile, every kilowatt hour, forever stored in a Postgres database on your own hardware, visualized in genuinely gorgeous Grafana dashboards. It has been at this for 7 years, and by deployment count, it\'s one'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the most run self-hosted apps in the entire car world. The maintainers ship one security warning with it that you need to hear. That\'s at the end. Here\'s the scale nobody sees from the star count. 28.2 million Docker pulls. Not'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bad for a repository sitting at a modest 8,000 internet four stars. That gap tells you exactly what kind of project this is. Not a hype cycle, but infrastructure. The quiet kind of'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'software that tens of thousands of garages, closets, and Raspberry Pis simply run every day for years without anyone tweeting about it. TeslaMate was created by Adrian Comp in 2019. 1,284 commits of his are still in the tree.'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And here\'s a health signal most projects never manage. A real maintainer succession. Today, it\'s led by Jakob Lichtefeld out of Stuttgart. Fittingly, car country. I\'ll carry a ton under a community organization with 236'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contributors behind it. The founder built it, the community carried it forward, and nobody had to fork anything or fight over a name. That is exactly how open source is supposed to age. Here\'s what you actually get. Grafana'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dashboards, dozens of them. Pre-built, no assembly required. This is the drive view. Your route on an open street map, speed, elevation, battery percentage, outside temperature, efficiency, every drive you\'ve ever taken, logged at high'

[01:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'precision and queryable forever. There are dashboards for charging sessions, charge costs, efficiency versus temperature, trips, software updates, even a map of everywhere the car has been. If the car measured it, you can'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'graph it and slice it by week, season, or tire set. The full feature list is long, so here are the highlights. High precision drive and charge logging. Charge tracking with actual cost'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'accounting. Plug in your electricity rates, including time of use tariffs, and know what every charge really costs you. Battery health and degradation over the life of the car. Geofencing, so home, work, and the supermarket get named'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automatically. MQTT output, which makes Home Assistant, Node-RED, and Telegram integrations nearly effortless. Multiple vehicles on one account. And if you\'re paying for TeslaFi right now,'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s a direct importer. Your entire history moves with you. Setup is the classic compose file. Four services, TeslaMate itself, Postgres for storage, Grafana for the dashboards, and Mosquito for MQTT.'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'One Docker compose up, sign in with your Tesla account, and logging begins. It runs happily on a Raspberry Pi, any 64-bit model from the Pi 3 onward, or whatever box is already humming in your'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'closet. 1 GB of RAM gets you started, two is comfortable. The difference this makes day-to-day, the Tesla app is built for right now. It shows the current charge, maybe your last trip, and then history'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just evaporates. No degradation curve, no cost ledger, and what data exists lives in someone else\'s cloud on someone else\'s retention schedule. With TeslaMate, every drive and every'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'charge since day one sits in your own database. You can answer real questions. What did charging actually cost me last winter? How much range has this battery genuinely lost in two years? Did that software update hurt my efficiency? The'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'app can\'t tell you. Your database can. Now, the question every Tesla owner asks first, won\'t constant polling keep the car awake and drain my battery overnight. This is where Tesla Mate\'s most impressive engineering lies. It\'s sleep'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'logic watches for the car trying to nod off and backs away immediately. No additional vampire drain isn\'t marketing copy. It\'s a headline feature the project has tuned for 7 years. You keep the data without paying for it in range.'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Owners obsess over exactly this and the community\'s verdict after all these years is trust. And this is the dashboard that sells it for most people. Battery health, usable capacity over time, projected versus actual range,'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'degradation plotted across the entire life of the car, you know, real measurements from your real battery, not form anecdotes or a dealer\'s shrug. When you\'re deciding whether to buy out a lease, sell the'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'car, or just settle an argument about charging to 100%, this one chart is worth the entire setup. The documentation deserves its own shout-out. Install guides for Docker and NixOS, hardened remote access setups'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with Traefik or Apache, TLS, and basic auth included. Integration guides for Home Assistant, MQTT, and Node-RED, importers from Teslify and Tesla API Scraper, and an FAQ that answers the'

