[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The repository was pushed to this very day and the project carries an Open SSF best practices badge, a security posture most hobby projects never even attempt. Boring, steady, trustworthy, the three best words in self-hosting. 7 years in'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the pace is accelerating. Adrian Camp started the the in March 2019. Now look at just the last 9 months. Version 2.2 in November, 3.0 in February, 4.0 in June. A release that resolved the owner'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'API token errors that had been breaking Tesla loggers everywhere at the cost of finally retiring 32-bit arm support. Three major versions in 8 months this deep into a project\'s life is rare air. Now, the fine print starting with the'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'warning the maintainers published themselves. There are fake TeslaMate websites and unofficial mobile apps in the wild distributing modified builds that can steal your Tesla credentials. Only install from the official repository and official docs. github.com'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'TeslaMate org and docs teslamate.org both linked below. Second, your Tesla API tokens are effectively keys to your car. TeslaMate encrypts them at rest,'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but the docs are explicit. Keep the instance on your home network and reach it remotely through a VPN or tunnel like Tailscale or Cloudflare tunnel rather than exposing it raw to the internet. Third, you need an always-on machine.'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '64-bit a Pi 3 or newer works. And it\'s AGPL 3.0 with no telemetry. Your driving data goes nowhere you didn\'t put it. So, if there\'s a Tesla in your driveway and'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
